course: dictionary
lesson: 8867
title: Shorn and its many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Shorn is one of these words. In «Farm sheep grow wool all the time and must be shorn every year», shorn means the wool has been cut off.
explain: Shorn can also mean having lost something. Shorn of something means it has been taken away. A king shorn of his crown no longer has his crown, so the word shows a big loss.
explain: Shorn can describe hair too. Shorn hair is cut very short. Shorn is a bit old or formal, so everyday talk often says sheared or had their hair cut. To check a meaning, swap in a simpler word and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Shorn means having had wool or hair cut off, often with big scissors or clippers.
fact: Shorn of something means having lost it, as in «shorn of his power».
fact: Shorn hair is cut very short.
fact: Shorn is the past participle of shear: shear, shears, sheared, shorn, shearing.
fact: Sheep in Australia, New Zealand and the UK are shorn each year so their wool can be used for clothes.
exercise: «Farm sheep must be shorn every year.» What does shorn mean here? | *Had their wool cut off | Were lost on the hills | Were painted bright colours | Had a long nap in the sun
exercise: «The king was shorn of his power.» What does shorn of mean? | *Having lost something | Being very tall | Having found a new friend | Being covered in snow
exercise: Lily says sheared is the everyday word. Which everyday words could replace shorn in «shorn hair»? | *cut very short | cut very long | painted | curled up
test: «Shorn hair» means hair that is what? | Very long and curly | Dyed bright blue | *Cut very short | Tied up in a bun
test: «In the story, the hero had shorn hair.» Which word could replace shorn with the same meaning? | Grown | *Cut | Tangled | Brushed
exam: In «a sheep called Shrek in New Zealand escaped shearing for six years», what does shearing mean? | *Cutting the wool off | Running away from farms | Sleeping in the sun | Painting the wool
